    {
      "id": "cp-science-diffusion",
      "family": "explanation",
      "answer": "No, random molecular motion does not stop.\n\nEven when two regions of water have the same salt concentration, the molecules in each region are still moving randomly. The salt concentration being equal means that the average number of dissolved salt ions per unit volume is the same in both regions. It does not mean that the individual ions are at rest or that their random motion has ceased.\n\nIn fact, the random motion of the ions continues in both regions. The salt concentration being equal is a statistical property that reflects the average behavior of many ions, not the motion of any single ion.",
      "grade": null,
      "max_new_tokens": 512,
