      "loop_flag": false,
      "combined_loop_flag": false
    },
    {
      "id": "cp-science-friction",
      "family": "explanation",
      "answer": "No, the book\u2019s kinetic energy has not disappeared. It has been transformed into other forms.\n\nWhen the book slides, friction between the book and the table does work on the book. That work removes kinetic energy from the book and transfers it to the table. The energy ends up as:\n\n- Heat (thermal energy) in the table and the book, due to microscopic vibrations of atoms and molecules.\n- A tiny amount of sound (acoustic energy) from the rubbing.\n- A very small amount of deformation energy if the surfaces deform slightly.\n\nThe total energy of the system (book + table + surroundings) is conserved; the book\u2019s kinetic energy is just converted into these other forms.",
      "grade": null,
